#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068433 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87636457-bdd5-53dd-9137-c070a4e12e84" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Discriminant of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "360239d4-c271-52f3-8d25-ed47e7dd52f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-10610" :question/problem "Calculate the discriminant of the quadratic equation $-2x^2+5x-1=0$." :question/worked-solution "We have $a=-2$, $b=5$, and $c=-1$. So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (5)^{2} - 4(-2)(-1) \\\\\n&= 25 - 8 \\\\\n&= 17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068434 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "926a6ba8-ec05-568a-9c13-c9f4d096e89f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Discriminant to Determine the Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "f667bd43-407c-55a9-9821-67a0c1b11a96" :question/math-academy-id "e-10611" :question/problem "Consider the equation $-x^2-x+1=0$. How many distinct real solutions does it have?" :question/worked-solution "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=-1$, $b=-1$, and $c=1$, so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-1)^{2} - 4(-1)(1) \\\\\n&= 1 + 4 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is positive $(\\mathcal{D}>0)$, the equation has two distinct solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068435 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "452d826b-4cb3-56e1-989a-b025b5d5c955" :knowledge-point/title "Choosing a Parameter to Force a Desired Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "5d4530b7-c6df-583f-b98f-8a8f93203295" :question/math-academy-id "e-10612" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $x^2+2x+k=0$ has no real solutions." :question/worked-solution "The equation has no real solutions if its discriminant is negative. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is negative.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=1$, $b=2$, and $c=k$, so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (2)^{2} - 4(1)(k) \\\\\n&= 4 - 4k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}D & < 0 \\\\ 4 - 4k & < 0 \\\\ 4 & < 4k \\\\ 1 & < k \\\\ k & > 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have no real solutions if $k>1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "adc75098-5640-5e81-a0bc-003fa20c090c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadratic Equations With Rational Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "b92b9a39-e29a-532f-ba2c-f935773d0bf7" :question/math-academy-id "e-10616" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation $x^2+2x -2 = 0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\n2. The equation has only rational solutions.\n3. The equation has only irrational solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $a=1$, $b=2$, and $c=-2$. So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n$D|= b^{2} - 4ac; = (2)^{2} - 4(1)(-2); = 4 + 8; = 12$\nAnd $12$ is *not* a perfect square.\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. First, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is not a perfect square, the equation must have two irrational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "452d826b-4cb3-56e1-989a-b025b5d5c955" :knowledge-point/title "Choosing a Parameter to Force a Desired Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24a9b789-abfb-5cbd-9e43-cbbe69e980a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-10318" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $-15{x}^{2}+kx-60=0$  has one distinct real solution." :question/worked-solution "The equation has one distinct real solution if its discriminant is zero. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is zero.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=-15,$ $b=k,$ and $c=-60,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(k)^{2}-4(-15)(-60) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}-3\\,600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=0 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3600 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =3600 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{3600} \\\\ k & =\\pm 60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have one distinct real solution if $k=\\pm 60.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "926a6ba8-ec05-568a-9c13-c9f4d096e89f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Discriminant to Determine the Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96eb4e16-d3f8-5bec-99cb-02da24948e7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-112849" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation $4{x}^{2}-12x+9=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?" :question/worked-solution "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=4,$ $b=-12,$ and $c=9,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-12)^{2}-4(4)(9) \\\\  & =144-144 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is zero $(D=0),$ the equation has one distinct real solution."}]}
